{
  "statement": "Compute-threshold licensing reduces catastrophic risk only when the gate selects on demonstrated safety and accountability, not on scale. Set the trigger to a raw compute headcount and you build a moat that entrenches OpenAI, Google DeepMind, and Anthropic without buying a single unit of safety. Condition the license on independent verification, a harm-indexed financial floor, and a regulator's power to halt, and the same threshold becomes the rare frontier-safety mechanism that is auditable rather than aspirational. That is the whole argument. The rest is enforcement mechanics.\n\nCompute is the one lever that is actually trackable today. Leading-edge fabrication sits in a handful of firms — TSMC, ASML for the lithography, a short list of others. The US export controls already key on advanced GPU and lithography flows, and the October 2023 executive order set a reporting trigger at 10^26 floating-point operations of training compute. You cannot inspect a model's goals or predict its behavior under distribution shift, but you can count the chips. That asymmetry is what makes compute the enforcement backbone. I carry one caveat honestly: algorithmic efficiency gains and distributed training may erode this chokepoint, and how long it holds is a question this debate has to resolve, not a fact I get to assume.\n\nA compute count alone is a barrier to entry, not a safety gate. The license price must be accountability — mandatory reporting, independent verification, and real penalties for fraud — so the gate selects on who can demonstrate safety and bear the downside, not on who is already large. This is how entrenchment stops being a fatal objection and becomes a calibration parameter I am willing to be held to. The FAA and FDA certify against published standards and prosecute misrepresentation; automotive crash-test regimes work the same way. I will not pretend the hard part is solved: verifiable frontier-safety benchmarks remain immature, and that is the design frontier, not a settled science.\n\nThe enforcement half of any license regime collapses without precautionary-halt authority shielded from liability. Regulators face an asymmetry — caution gets them blamed for slowing progress, inaction gets forgiven when the failure looks like an accident. The Boeing 737 MAX killed 346 people because the FAA delegated certification to the manufacturer and no one with authority stopped the line. FAA airworthiness directives and FDA market withdrawals work because sovereign immunity lets an inspector ground a fleet without personal ruin. Absent that power keyed to the compute trigger, thresholds become paperwork. I grant the shield is necessary, not sufficient — budget limits and regulatory capture drive under-enforcement too.\n\nThe burden shifts to the deployer only for capabilities that cannot be rolled back at scale — not for deployment in general. Ask the accelerationist the only question that matters: what is your rollback plan, and who has the authority to trigger it? A staged-deployment model with continuous monitoring is not my enemy; compute-gated licensing is the floor beneath it, not a substitute for it. Remove the floor and you are betting that ungoverned success is safety. It is not. It is luck.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🎛️", "tooltip": "The compute threshold is a dial to be calibrated on accountability, not a wall that only the tallest firms can climb."},
    {"symbol": "🛑", "tooltip": "A license without a halt button is paperwork — the 737 MAX proved that delegated certification with no one to stop the line kills people."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "Compute-threshold licensing reduces catastrophic risk only when the gate selects on demonstrated safety and accountability, not on scale.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Set the trigger to a raw compute headcount and you build a moat that entrenches OpenAI, Google DeepMind, and Anthropic without buying a single unit of safety.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "You cannot inspect a model's goals or predict its behavior under distribution shift, but you can count the chips.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "The Boeing 737 MAX killed 346 people because the FAA delegated certification to the manufacturer and no one with authority stopped the line.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Ask the accelerationist the only question that matters: what is your rollback plan, and who has the authority to trigger it?", "targets": [], "relationship": ""}
  ]
}

