  "statement": "Safetyist made the strongest possible case against a naive compute gate, and I want that on the record before I build. Safetyist said plainly that a raw compute headcount trigger builds a moat around OpenAI, Google DeepMind, and Anthropic without buying a single unit of safety. That's correct, and it's the most important sentence anyone has said in this debate. Safetyist also made the honest observation that you cannot inspect a model's goals or predict its behavior under distribution shift, but you can count the chips. I take that seriously. Countability is real, and a regulator who needs an enforceable trigger tomorrow reaches for the thing they can measure.\n\nHere's where the evidence splits from Safetyist. Countability is not risk-relevance. A FLOP ceiling — a limit on the raw arithmetic operations used to train a model — over-covers benign large runs and under-covers the capability jumps that actually carry catastrophic risk. DeepSeek-V3 reached frontier-adjacent performance for a reported training cost near $5.6 million, roughly a tenth of what a GPT-4-class run was estimated to cost. Distillation and mixture-of-experts architectures keep decoupling capability from raw chip count. So a compute threshold set at 10^26 operations, the trigger written into the October 2023 executive order, catches the next big-budget training run while missing the efficient architecture that does the dangerous thing on a fraction of the hardware. You buy measurability and you pay for it with a moat.\n\nThe gate that actually cuts catastrophic risk keys on demonstrated behavior, verified by an independent third-party auditor, tied to strict liability at deployment. Look at what killed 346 people in the two 737 MAX crashes. The FAA delegated certification back to Boeing and let the manufacturer grade its own homework. That was captured self-certification failing — not the existence of a gate. The fix is neither self-attestation nor a scale license only a billion-dollar firm can clear. It's an audit a garage developer can also pass, because the auditor tests what the system does, not how many H100s trained it.\n\nPut the gate at deployment and inference, not upstream at training, so open-weight and small developers aren't foreclosed before they ship. This answers Safetyist's fair question — who holds rollback authority — with a concrete locus instead of a dodge. Product-liability law already apportions responsibility between a component maker and the integrator who ships the final product; frontier AI can borrow that structure. Responsibility lands on the operator who deploys, with threshold-based escalation to a human authority at defined risk boundaries. I'll own the hard part directly: reliably defining that risk boundary is unsolved, and I won't pretend a downstream-liability regime is costless or that audits can't be gamed. Independent, transparently-funded auditors are the price of admission, and I'll take a narrow audit gate only for objectively-scoped irreversible-harm systems.\n\nThe topic asks for two things at once — cut catastrophic risk and avoid entrenching incumbents — and a scale-gate resolves them in opposite directions. It maxes out one by handing three labs a moat on the other. Every quarter the trigger keys on chip count instead of behavior, capability stays locked inside those three firms and the developers who'd point it at pancreatic cancer or antibiotic discovery get priced out at the door. That's not a rhetorical flourish. It's an opportunity cost the countability frame is structurally built to hide, and someone has to say out loud who pays it.",
