d who has standing to challenge a clean bill of health. Build your enforcement joint out of the exact material that already failed, and you get the same fracture.\n\nNow the population nobody at this table has named. Tie licensing to deployment liability and you price only the harms that get litigated. Experience-rated premiums run off loss history, and loss history runs off lawsuits. The most common AI injuries are diffuse, small-dollar, and invisible to the person harmed — a denied loan, a screening filter that drops a résumé. Survey after survey shows people do not know an automated decision touched them, let alone that they could sue. That harm never enters the pricing engine, so the gate never sees it, so the regime never deters it. Catastrophic-tail focus and small-diffuse-harm blindness are two symptoms of the same design.\n\nWhich leaves the moat. The Safetyist blamed the compute number, but the number is not the barrier — the compliance infrastructure is. Fixed audit and liaison costs are regressive to firm size in every licensed industry on record, from pharmaceuticals to broker-dealers. A two-person lab cannot staff an audit-response team or absorb months of pre-deployment review; a $90-billion incumbent barely notices. Switch the trigger from gigaflops to behavior and that cost structure does not move an inch. So the real conditions are not which metric anchors the gate. They are: who funds the auditor, who can contest a clean audit, who eats the liability, and who gets injured without ever knowing it happened — and I have not heard either speaker answer one of them.",
