  "statement": "The Accelerationist made the sharpest point in this room, and it deserves stating cleanly: a compute gate set at 10^26 operations catches the next big-budget training run and misses the lean architecture that does the dangerous thing on a fraction of the hardware. That's correct. Counting chips tells you about a firm's budget, not its model's behavior. So the Accelerationist's fix — key the gate on demonstrated behavior, verify it with an independent third-party auditor, tie it to strict liability at deployment — is the strongest design on the table. The Safetyist is also right that a raw compute headcount builds a drawbridge that OpenAI, Google DeepMind, and Anthropic pull up behind them without buying a single unit of safety.\n\nHere's what would have to be true for both of them to be right: the substitute metric has to be un-gameable, and the audit has to be honest. Neither speaker has shown either. The Accelerationist proved that efficiency defeats a compute gate — distillation, algorithmic tricks, inference-time compute all decouple capability from raw operations. Run that same logic forward. What stops a lab from pruning its training data and tuning to the evaluation set so the model passes the exact benchmark the auditor scores? Swap compute for behavior and you have not fixed the trigger problem. You have relocated it to a benchmark that becomes the thing developers optimize around instead of the danger it was meant to catch.\n\nBoth speakers reached for the 737 MAX. Read the case again. The FAA delegated certification authority back to Boeing engineers whose paychecks depended on shipping the plane, and 346 people died in two crashes. That is not an argument for an audit-based licensing regime. That is a demonstration of what one becomes by default. The Accelerationist wants an \"independent\" third-party auditor and skips the three questions that decide whether independence is real: who pays the auditor, who sets the scope of the review, and who has standing to challenge a clean bill of health. Build your enforcement joint out of the exact material that already failed, and you get the same fracture.\n\nNow the population nobody at this table has named. Tie licensing to deployment liability and you price only the harms that get litigated. Experience-rated premiums run off loss history, and loss history runs off lawsuits. The most common AI injuries are diffuse, small-dollar, and invisible to the person harmed — a denied loan, a screening filter that drops a résumé. Survey after survey shows people do not know an automated decision touched them, let alone that they could sue. That harm never enters the pricing engine, so the gate never sees it, so the regime never deters it. Catastrophic-tail focus and small-diffuse-harm blindness are two symptoms of the same design.\n\nWhich leaves the moat. The Safetyist blamed the compute number, but the number is not the barrier — the compliance infrastructure is. Fixed audit and liaison costs are regressive to firm size in every licensed industry on record, from pharmaceuticals to broker-dealers. A two-person lab cannot staff an audit-response team or absorb months of pre-deployment review; a $90-billion incumbent barely notices. Switch the trigger from gigaflops to behavior and that cost structure does not move an inch. So the real conditions are not which metric anchors the gate. They are: who funds the auditor, who can contest a clean audit, who eats the liability, and who gets injured without ever knowing it happened — and I have not heard either speaker answer one of them.",
