{
  "statement": "Here is the strongest version of what Safetyist is proposing, and it's a real advance: don't attach the auditor's paycheck, the audit's scope, or the right to challenge a clean report to the company being certified. Write all three into statute so they flow from a public source, index the audit burden to how irreversible the harm is rather than how big the training run is, and hold third-party standing-to-sue in reserve as the check that survives even if the auditor gets partially captured. That is a genuinely better design than a raw compute ceiling, and I said as much when I argued the moat is compliance-capacity cost, not the FLOP number. Now the uncomfortable part. The Organization Designation Authorization that let Boeing sign off on the 737 MAX was not a loophole around Congress. It was a statute — 49 U.S.C. 44702 — the exact instrument Safetyist is now proposing to use as the firewall.\n\nSo let me answer the falsifiability demand directly, because I refuse to hide behind the claim that no gate can ever work. Statute is not a wall against capture; it is the paperwork through which capture becomes legal. The three vectors — who funds, who scopes, who has standing — are not settled once when the bill passes. They get re-litigated every single appropriations cycle and every appointment, and those are the two rooms where incumbents already dominate. The peer-reviewed aviation work on the AI Act makes the point without meaning to: aviation had a mature incident-classification framework and statutory certification authority, and the delegation still hollowed out from the inside because the money and the scope drifted back toward the certified party over time. Show me a severed statutory audit gate — public funding plus live third-party standing — that survived one full appropriations cycle under sustained industry lobbying without its budget starved or its scope narrowed. Produce that case and I change my assessment. Absent it, the burden sits on whoever claims severance holds, not on the person who has watched it fail with 346 bodies attached.\n\nThere is a deeper contradiction in the two answers, and it's between them, not inside either one. Safetyist leans on third-party standing-to-contest as the backstop that survives partial capture — the person with standing can drag a captured auditor into daylight. But Safetyist also adopted my invisibility point: most people injured by an automated decision never learn a model touched them. A loan denied, a resume screened out, a benefit quietly downgraded. Standing cannot reach a plaintiff who does not know they are a plaintiff. So the backstop covers the litigants who can already see the injury — corporate competitors, sophisticated parties, the well-resourced — and misses the diffuse population the whole precautionary case was built to protect. The two moves cancel. You cannot cite invisibility as the reason for a pre-deployment gate and then hand the enforcement job to a lawsuit that requires the invisible to become visible.\n\nWatch what happens to the hazard-tiering idea, because it's the same trick the accelerationist already ran on compute, aimed at a fresh target. Move the trigger from headcount to irreversibility class and the classification step becomes the thing developers engineer around. This is not speculation — it's the documented pattern where teams prune training data to dodge a domain-specific threshold. Restructure the deployment so it lands one hazard tier down, split a capability across two systems that each read as reversible in isolation, and you have relocated the trigger again, not fixed it. The EU AI Act already tiers by risk category, and the fight over which systems count as high-risk is exactly where the lobbying money is going right now. Every gate creates a boundary, and every boundary becomes a place to stand just on the safe side of.\n\nSo here is the question I'd put to both of you before anyone writes 'statutory' into a bill as if the word does the work. If the specific delegation that killed 346 people was itself a statute, drafted and appropriated and appointed through the same process you're trusting to sever the vectors this time — what, concretely, is different about your bill except that it hasn't been lobbied yet?",
